    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras are positioned for a high-ceiling offensive output tonight, led by the elite pairing of **Trea Turner** (80.42) and **Yordan Alvarez** (77.22). **Trea Turner** remains our primary catalyst against P\u00e9rez, benefiting from a soft 4.66 xERA matchup and a prime spot in the #2 hole that maximizes his stolen base potential\u2014the scarcest commodity in our current 5x5 build. **Yordan Alvarez** continues to operate as an offensive engine; despite a neutral park adjustment, his elite .450 xwOBA against Pfaadt makes him a locked-in start. We are also leaning heavily on **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (74.72) and **Lars Nootbaar** (65.16); both project as high-efficiency plays today, with **Lars Nootbaar** specifically catching a massive tailwind from the 6.19 xERA of Pecko, while **Pete Crow-Armstrong** earns his score through an aggressive #1 lineup placement and favorable platoon dynamics.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Junior Caminero** (53.59) and **Carson Benge** (55.87). While **Junior Caminero** was projected to provide value, the matchup against deGrom forced a 19% reduction in his skill rating, nearly pushing him out of the UTIL spot. We opted to keep him in the lineup based on his #3 order position, though his proximity to the 50-point threshold warrants caution. Conversely, **Kazuma Okamoto** (57.84) held steady despite a mediocre matchup against Lugo, as his reliable base projection anchors our infield. Looking at our bench, **Caleb Durbin**, **Colt Keith**, and **Christian Yelich** remain unavailable, leaving our depth restricted as **Casey Schmitt** continues to nurse his injury. We must monitor the official confirmation for **Gary Sanchez** (60.98), who is currently our most efficient catcher, though his production is highly dependent on him maintaining the #8 spot against Abbott. Should any late-scratch lineup changes occur, we are prepared to pivot to keep our game-cap efficiency high.",
